/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A rolling bearing is not designed from a blank page; it is chosen from a catalogue.
   - The life equation of Chapter 5.6 links C, P and life.
   - Creq is the required basic dynamic load rating in N; P is the equivalent dynamic load in N; L₁₀ is the rating life in million revolutions; p = 3 for ball bearings and 10/3 for roller bearings.
   - The life hidden inside C is counted in million revolutions, so the hours must be converted before they enter the formula.
   - n is the speed in rev/min; L10h is the required life in hours; 60 changes rev/min into rev per hour and 10⁶ changes revolutions into millions.
   - Choose the smallest bearing in the size range whose C is at least Creq.
   - Bearings are rated for one kind of load: pure radial, steady, applied through the centre.
   - Fr and Fa are the radial and axial loads in N; X is the radial factor and Y the axial factor, read from the table for that bearing type.
   - For a single-row deep-groove ball bearing the factors depend on Fa/C₀, the thrust compared with the static rating.
   - Because Fa/C₀ needs the C₀ of a bearing not yet chosen, selection is a short loop: assume a bearing, read its C₀, find e and Y, compute P and Creq, and accept the bearing if its C is at least Creq.
*/
