/* book Worked example: Q: A deep-groove ball bearing of 45 mm bore carries Fr = 5 kN and Fa = 2 kN at 1200 rev/min and must give L10h = 10 000 h in ordinary machinery (s₀ = 2). Two bearings of that bore are available: A with C = 33.2 kN, C₀ = 21.6 kN, and B with C = 55.3 kN, C₀ = 31.5 kN. Select the bearing.
   1. Life in million revolutions: L₁₀ = 60 × 1200 × 10 000/10⁶ = 720. For a ball bearing L₁₀1/3 = 7201/3 = 8.963.
   2. Try A: Fa/C₀ = 2/21.6 = 0.0926, between the rows 0.084 and 0.11, so e = 0.287 and Y = 1.517. Fa/Fr = 0.4 > e.
   3. P = 0.56 × 5000 + 1.517 × 2000 = 2800 + 3034 = 5834 N, and Creq = 5834 × 8.963 = 52 290 N. A has only 33.2 kN: reject.
   4. Try B: Fa/C₀ = 2/31.5 = 0.0635, between 0.056 and 0.084: e = 0.265 and Y = 1.71 − 0.268 × 0.16 = 1.667. Fa/Fr = 0.4 > e.
   5. P = 0.56 × 5000 + 1.667 × 2000 = 2800 + 3334 = 6134 N, and Creq = 6134 × 8.963 = 54 980 N ≤ 55 300 N: B passes.
   6. Life check for B: L₁₀ = (55 300/6134)³ = 9.015³ = 732.6 million revolutions = 732.6 × 10⁶/72 000 = 10 175 h ≥ 10 000 h.
   7. Static check: 0.6 × 5000 + 0.5 × 2000 = 4000 N is less than Fr, so P₀ = 5000 N, and s₀ = 31.5/5 = 6.3 ≥ 2. Finally confirm that the catalogue limiting speed of B is above 1200 rev/min.
   Answer: Bearing B: P = 6134 N, Creq ≈ 55.0 kN against C = 55.3 kN, life ≈ 10 175 h and s₀ = 6.3. */
