/* book Worked example: Q: A pressure vessel cover is sealed with a flat compressed-fibre gasket, inside diameter 300 mm and outside diameter 320 mm, with m = 2.00 and y = 11 MPa. The design pressure is 2 MPa and the allowable bolt stress is 138 MPa. Find the seating load, the operating load, the bolt area and a suitable set of bolts.
   1. Gasket width N = (320 − 300)/2 = 10 mm, so b₀ = N/2 = 5 mm. Since 5 ≤ 6.4 mm, b = 5 mm and G = mean diameter = (300 + 320)/2 = 310 mm.
   2. Seating load: Wm2 = π b G y = π × 5 × 310 × 11 = 53 564 N = 53.6 kN.
   3. Pressure end force: H = (π/4) G² p = 0.7854 × 310² × 2 = 150 954 N.
   4. Gasket load under pressure: Hp = 2π b G m p = 2π × 5 × 310 × 2.00 × 2 = 38 956 N.
   5. Operating load: Wm1 = H + Hp = 150 954 + 38 956 = 189 910 N = 189.9 kN. This is larger than Wm2, so it governs.
   6. Bolt area: Am = 189 910/138 = 1376 mm². With 16 bolts, each needs 1376/16 = 86.0 mm².
   7. M12 has a stress area of 84.3 mm², too small; M14 has 115 mm². Choose 16 bolts of M14, total 1840 mm².
   Answer: Wm2 = 53.6 kN, Wm1 = 189.9 kN (governs), Am = 1376 mm²; 16 bolts of M14 (1840 mm²) will do. */
